---
title: "Front-matter reference"
description: "Every top-level front-matter key, the closed vocabulary behind did-you-mean, and a fully annotated example."
---

Every `.tmd` document can open with a YAML **front-matter** block: a `---`
fenced header that sets the document's metadata, output, and behaviour. This page
is the full vocabulary, every top-level key Taliesin reads, plus the nested blocks
(`execute:`, `listing:`, `hero:`, `prose-lint:`) and their children. For
project-wide settings (a site's navbar, footer, chapter order) see the
[Configuration reference](configuration.tmd); for per-cell `#|` options see the
[Cell-options reference](cell-options.tmd).

A minimal block is just a title:

```yaml
---
title: "My post"
---
```

Taliesin validates this block against its **own** key set. Any key it
does not implement, whether a typo (`treme:`) or a term it never honored
(`title-block-banner:`), is reported with a "did you mean …" hint (see
[Closed vocabulary](#closed-vocabulary) below). Validation only **warns**: the
document still renders, and each warning is located so you can click it straight to
the offending line in the live preview.

## Every top-level key

The keys group into eight concerns. Types: a *string* is plain text; a *bool* is
`true`/`false`; a *list* is a YAML sequence; an *include* is a path, a `{ text: … }`
or `{ file: … }` map, or a list of those.

### Identity and metadata

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `title` | string | The document title. Drives `<title>`, the title block, OpenGraph/social meta, and the nav/listing label |
| `subtitle` | string | A secondary line shown under the title in the title block |
| `author` | string, list, or list of maps | Author name(s). A string or list of strings is a plain byline; the [structured form](#sec-authors) adds affiliations, ORCIDs and links |
| `date` | string | Publication date (shown in the title block; sorts a `listing:`) |
| `description` | string | Summary used for the `<meta name="description">` and social cards |
| `lang` | string | BCP-47 language tag for `<html lang>` (default `en`) |
| `categories` | list | Tags for the page; feed `listing:` cards and the category filter row |

### Output, format, and theming

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `format` | string | `html` (default), `deck` (a slide deck), or a `<name>-deck` variant. **Its sub-keys are not validated** |
| `theme` | string | `light`, `dark`, a `.css`/`.scss` file path, or an installed `_extensions/<name>/` theme bundle. Unspecified (or any custom theme) follows the OS via `prefers-color-scheme`, falling back to light; only an explicit `light` (or `default`)/`dark` forces that mode |
| `css` | string \| list \| map | Extra stylesheet(s) inlined into the page: a path, a `{ text: … }` / `{ file: … }` map, or a list of those |
| `page-layout` | string | `full` widens the content column (good for listing indexes); the default is the reading-width column |
| `image` | string | The image on a `listing:` card; the social-card image is now auto-generated per page |
| `image-alt` | string | The `alt` text on this page's `listing:` card image (and its social card). Not rendered on the page itself. **Set it whenever you set `image:`** — an `image:` with no `image-alt:` warns, since only you know whether the picture carries meaning. If it is purely decorative say so explicitly with `image-alt: ""`, which silences the warning and emits an empty `alt` |
| `draft` | bool | `true` keeps the page (or book chapter) out of a `build`/`publish` entirely (no output, nav, listing, or prev/next), and the build reports it as "not published". It is also out of scope for `check` — a page that does not ship should not fail a pre-flight, and `check` names the drafts it held back so the verdict is not read as wider than it is. `preview` still shows it, badged, and **does** lint it, so you see its diagnostics where you are writing. Ignored on an `{{< embed >}}` target |
| `title-block-style` | string | `none` keeps the title metadata (still drives `<title>`, OpenGraph, nav) but hides the visible `<h1>` title block |

### Deck chrome

Both are read only by a slide deck (`format: deck`) and ignored on an ordinary page.
See [Slide decks](../using/formats.tmd#slide-decks).

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `footer` | string | Small footer text repeated on every slide |
| `logo` | string | An image path shown in the slide's bottom-left corner; resolved and copied like any other slide image |

### Raw-HTML includes

Each of these injects trusted, verbatim markup. The value can be a path, a
`{ text: … }` or `{ file: … }` map, or a list of those.

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `include-in-header` | include | Markup added inside `<head>` |
| `include-before-body` | include | Markup added at the start of `<body>` |
| `include-after-body` | include | Markup added at the end of `<body>` |

### Contents, bibliography, and citations

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `toc` | bool | `true` adds a live table of contents (and enables the Cmd-K search palette). No effect in a book chapter: a book navigates by its Chapters drawer |
| `bibliography` | string \| list | A `.bib` file (or list of them) enabling `[@key]` citations and an auto-generated References section |
| `csl` | string | **Recognized, but not supported: `check` warns and the key does nothing.** References always render in the built-in IEEE style. It stays recognized only so a `csl:` key is never mistaken for a typo'd `css:`; delete it |
| `doi` | string | This page's own DOI, for the Google Scholar `citation_doi` tag. Write it however you copied it — `10.5281/zenodo.1234`, `https://doi.org/10.5281/…` and `doi:10.5281/…` all mean the same thing and are normalised to the bare form |

### The resource row

The Paper / arXiv / Code row a research page hands its reader, plus the badges beside
it. See [`links:`](#links-the-resource-row).

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `links` | list | Resource links under the byline. A bare URL infers its own label and icon; a `{ text:, href:, icon: }` map overrides either half |
| `venue` | string | Where the work appeared (`Proceedings of …`, `NeurIPS 2026`). Shown as a badge, and emitted as the Scholar `citation_conference_title` |
| `award` | string | An award badge shown beside the venue (`Best Paper`) |
| `acknowledgments` | string | Acknowledgments prose, rendered as an [appendix](#the-appendix) section. `acknowledgements` is the same key |
| `acknowledgements` | string | The British spelling of `acknowledgments`; identical in effect, and neither is a typo |

### Code execution

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `execute` | map | Document-wide defaults for code cells (`echo`/`include`/`cache`); see [`execute:`](#execute-document-wide-cell-defaults) |

### Site page blocks

These two turn a page in a site or book into something special. They are detailed
in their own sections below.

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `listing` | map | Turns the page into an index: a card per document under `contents:`. See [`listing:`](#listing-an-index-page) |
| `hero` | map | A hero banner at the top of a landing page (eyebrow, headline, lead, call-to-action buttons). See [`hero:`](#hero-a-landing-banner) |

### Prose linting

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `prose-lint` | bool \| map | Opt-in prose linter. `true` enables the built-in rules; a `{ banned: [...] }` map adds custom banned terms. See [`prose-lint:`](#prose-lint-the-prose-linter) |

### Theorem environments

| Key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `theorems` | map | Numbering for theorem environments: `shared:` (a list of kinds drawing on one counter) and `numbered:` (`true` / `false` / `unless-unique`). Numbering *scope* is deliberately not a key: an environment scopes to its numbered book chapter automatically. See [Theorems and proofs](../using/theorems.tmd) |

## `author:` (who wrote it) {#sec-authors}

The short spellings are what most pages want, and neither is going anywhere:

```yaml
author: "Ada Lovelace"                      # one author
author: ["Ada Lovelace", "Grace Hopper"]    # several
```

For a paper or project page, an author is more than a name. The **structured** form
takes a list of maps:

```yaml
author:
  - name: "Ada Lovelace"
    affiliation: "Analytical Engine Institute"
    orcid: "0000-0002-1825-0097"
    url: "https://example.org/ada"
    equal: true
  - name: "Charles Babbage"
    affiliation: ["Analytical Engine Institute", "Analytical Society"]
    equal: true
```

| Sub-key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | string | The name, as it should read in the byline. The only required part |
| `affiliation` | string or list | Institution(s). Written as **names**, not as index numbers |
| `orcid` | string | An ORCID; published as a resolvable `https://orcid.org/…` identifier |
| `url` | string | A personal page; makes the name a link in the byline |
| `email` | string | Reaches the metadata only. Deliberately *not* rendered as a `mailto:` link, since a published address is what harvesters read |
| `equal` | boolean | Marks an equal-contribution author (`*` in the byline, with a note under it) |
| `contribution` | string | What this person did. Collected into the [appendix](#the-appendix)'s Author Contributions section |

**You never write an affiliation number.** Every other tool in this space asks you to
keep a list of institutions and put `affiliation: 1` against each author. Taliesin reads
the institution's *name* and derives the superscripts from the order they first appear,
so two authors who name the same institution share a number automatically, there is no
second list to keep in sync, and reordering authors cannot silently re-attribute anyone.
In the example above both authors show `1`, and Babbage adds `2`.

One declaration feeds everything downstream that has to name a person: the byline, the
Google Scholar `citation_author` and `citation_author_institution` tags, the JSON-LD
`Person` and its `affiliation`, the Atom feed, and all three formats in the "Cite this"
box. A typo in a sub-key is a warning, not a silent drop.

## `links:` (the resource row) {#links-the-resource-row}

The row of artefact buttons under the byline — the one element every research page
keeps, because handing a reader the paper, the code and the data *is* the page's job.

```yaml
---
title: "On the Analytical Engine"
date: 2026-03-09
venue: "Proceedings of the Analytical Society"
award: "Best Paper"
links:
  - https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.01234
  - https://github.com/example/analytical-engine
  - { text: "Supplementary", href: "https://example.org/supplement.pdf" }
---
```

**A bare URL is the whole spelling.** The label and the icon are read off the URL, so
the three entries above render as **arXiv**, **Code** and **Supplementary** without
your writing the first two labels at all:

| The URL says | Label | |
|---|---|---|
| `arxiv.org` | arXiv | |
| `github.com`, `gitlab.com`, `codeberg.org` | Code | |
| `huggingface.co`, `zenodo.org`, `osf.io` | Data | |
| `doi.org` | DOI | |
| `youtube.com`, `youtu.be`, `vimeo.com` | Video | |
| anything ending `.pdf` | Paper | |
| anything else | the hostname | e.g. `mylab.ac.uk` |

An unrecognised host labels itself rather than guessing, which is why the override is
optional rather than required. When you do want one, a `{ text:, href:, icon: }` map
replaces either half on its own — `{ text: Our repo, href: https://github.com/… }`
keeps the inferred code icon. `icon:` takes one of `paper`, `code`, `data`, `video`
or `link`; the glyphs are bundled SVG, so the row renders with no network at all.

The row is also read by machines. A `venue:` becomes the Google Scholar
`citation_conference_title`, and an `arxiv.org` link supplies `citation_arxiv_id` —
derived from the link rather than declared again, since you already wrote the id.

This lives in the title block on purpose, and it is *not* part of
[`hero:`](#hero-a-landing-banner): a hero replaces the title block wholesale, so a
paper page would have to choose between its byline and its links.

## The appendix {#the-appendix}

Three things a paper owes its reader that prose tends to forget: who did what, who
made it possible, and where the citable record lives. Declare any of them and
Taliesin appends an appendix section after the references.

```yaml
---
title: "On the Analytical Engine"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.1825009
acknowledgments: "With thanks to the Analytical Society."
author:
  - name: "Grace Hopper"
    contribution: "Designed the study and wrote the analysis."
  - name: "Charles Babbage"
    contribution: "Built the engine and ran the experiments."
---
```

**A contribution is declared beside the name, not in a list of its own.** Every other
tool makes you write a separate block keyed by author name, which then has to match a
name you typed somewhere else — and silently drops the statement when the two
spellings drift apart. As an `author:` sub-key it cannot miss, and an author who
declares none simply contributes no row.

Acknowledgments are usually a paragraph, so write them as a YAML folded block when a
quoted one-liner gets unwieldy:

```yaml
acknowledgments: >
  We thank the reviewers for their patience, and the Analytical
  Society for access to the difference engine.
```

The `doi:` renders as a resolvable `doi.org` link and is the same key Google Scholar's
`citation_doi` reads, so the identifier is written once.

Nothing in the appendix reads a clock: there is no "accessed" date and no build
timestamp, so a rebuild of an unchanged page is byte-identical and the `_freeze/`
execution cache is never invalidated by it.

## `execute:` (document-wide cell defaults) {#execute-document-wide-cell-defaults}

A nested `execute:` block sets defaults for **every** code cell in the document. A
cell's own `#|` option always overrides the document default.

| Sub-key | Type | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `echo` | bool | `true` | `false` hides the source of every cell (the cell still runs and its output still shows) |
| `include` | bool | `true` | `false` hides both source and output of every cell (the cell still runs, so it keeps building kernel state) |
| `cache` | bool | `true` | `false` opts every cell out of the `_freeze/` execution cache (always re-run) |

```yaml
---
title: "Tables, not code"
execute:
  echo: false        # show outputs, hide every source listing
---
```

See the [Cell-options reference](cell-options.tmd) for the per-cell `#|` equivalents
that override these.

## `listing:` (an index page) {#listing-an-index-page}

A `listing:` block crawls a directory and renders a card or row per document it
finds, sorted and capped however you ask. It is how you build a blog index or a
projects page.

| Sub-key | Type | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `contents` | string | (required) | A single directory to crawl for documents (one directory string, not a list or glob). Without it, there is nothing to list |
| `id` | string | (none) | Fill a matching `::: {#id}` block in the page body instead of appending the listing to the end |
| `sort` | string | `"date desc"` | Sort order. Listings only ever key on `date`: the order is descending **unless the value contains `asc`** (so `"date asc"` sorts oldest-first). A non-date key like `"title asc"` is not honored, it still sorts by date |
| `type` | string | (list) | `grid` renders a card grid; anything else (or omitted) renders a stacked list |
| `max-items` | int | (none) | Cap the number of items shown |
| `categories` | bool | `false` | `true` renders a clickable category filter row above the listing |

```yaml
---
title: "Blog"
page-layout: full
listing:
  contents: posts        # crawl ./posts for documents
  type: grid             # grid of cards (vs. the default stacked list)
  sort: "date desc"      # newest first ("date asc" for oldest first)
  max-items: 12
  categories: true       # show a category filter row
---
```

::: {.callout-tip}
Set `id:` and place a `::: {#recent}` block where you want the listing to land, so
you can write an intro above it and the cards below it on the same page.
:::

## `hero:` (a landing banner) {#hero-a-landing-banner}

A `hero:` block puts a full-width banner at the top of a landing page: an eyebrow
label, a headline, a lead paragraph, and a row of call-to-action buttons.

| Sub-key | Type | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `eyebrow` | string | (none) | A small label above the headline |
| `headline` | string | (falls back to `title`) | The large banner heading |
| `lead` | string | (none) | A lead paragraph under the headline |
| `actions` | list | (none) | A list of `{ text, href, primary }` buttons; `primary: true` styles the button as the prominent action (an item missing `text` or `href` is dropped) |

```yaml
---
title: "Taliesin"
hero:
  eyebrow: "A dev server for .tmd"
  headline: "Render .tmd to HTML, fast."
  lead: "Click-to-source, block-level incremental updates, a warm kernel."
  actions:
    - { text: "Get started", href: "using/getting-started.tmd", primary: true }
    - { text: "Reference",   href: "reference/cli.tmd" }
---
```

## `prose-lint:` (the prose linter) {#prose-lint-the-prose-linter}

`prose-lint` is an opt-in linter that flags weak prose. It is **off by default**.
Set it to `true` to enable the built-in rules (doubled words like "the the", and
weasel words like "very", "really"), or give it a mapping with `banned:` to add
your own terms.

| Sub-key | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `banned` | list | Words to flag wherever they appear in prose (case-insensitive) |

```yaml
---
title: "An edited post"
prose-lint:
  banned: [utilize, leverage, synergy]
---
```

Findings are located warnings (click-to-source) and skip code, math, links, and
HTML, so only real prose is checked. `prose-lint: true` enables the built-in rules
without any custom banned list.

## Closed vocabulary and "did you mean" {#closed-vocabulary}

Taliesin's front-matter vocabulary is a **closed set**: the keys above are the only
ones it reads. Anything else warns, with a "did you mean …" hint offered when an
unknown key is within edit distance 2 of a known one, so `treme: dark` is caught and
nudged toward `theme:`. The same closed-vocabulary check covers the immediate
children of `execute:`, `listing:`, `hero:`, and `prose-lint:`.

Two deliberate exceptions:

- **`format:` sub-keys are not linted.** A format extension may add its own format
  options, so Taliesin leaves everything under `format:` alone rather than guess at a
  vocabulary it does not own.
- **`csl:` is recognized but not supported.** There is no CSL engine: references always
  render in the single built-in IEEE style. `check` warns on the key (it is not offered
  as a completion either), so the no-op is visible rather than silent. It stays in the
  known set on purpose: `css` is one edit away from `csl`, so dropping it would make the
  did-you-mean answer a citation-style key with "did you mean `css`?", which is worse
  advice than none.

## Editor autocomplete

Taliesin ships a JSON Schema for front matter so your editor's YAML language server
can autocomplete keys, show hovers, and flag unknown keys as you type. Drop the
schemas into your project and point a modeline at the front-matter one:

```sh
taliesin schema --out .tmd
```

```yaml
---
# yaml-language-server: $schema=.tmd/tali-frontmatter.schema.json
title: My post
---
```

The schema describes the same key set Taliesin validates against at render time, so
the editor and the renderer always agree. Front-matter validation needs an editor
that treats a `.tmd` file's `---` block as embedded YAML (for example the VS Code
YAML extension); see [Configuration](configuration.tmd) for the full setup.

## A fully annotated example

Putting it together, here is a front-matter block exercising many of the keys at
once, with a comment on each:

```yaml
---
# --- Identity / metadata ---
title: "Decoding the loss curve"
subtitle: "What the wiggles mean"
author: "Andreas Bogossian"
date: "2026-06-27"
description: "A close read of a training run."   # social card + <meta description>
lang: en                                          # <html lang> (default en)
categories: [machine-learning, training]          # listing tags + filter chips

# --- Output / format / theme ---
format: html                                      # html (default) or deck
theme: dark                                        # light / dark / file / extension (unspecified follows the OS)
page-layout: full                                 # widen the column (default: reading width)
css: extra.css                                     # inlined stylesheet(s)

# --- Social / images ---
image: cover.png                                   # listing-card image; social card on site/book builds
image-alt: "A loss curve trending down"           # alt text for this post's listing card image

# --- Contents / citations ---
toc: true                                          # live TOC + Cmd-K search
bibliography: refs.bib                              # enables [@key] + References

# --- Raw-HTML includes (trusted, verbatim) ---
include-in-header: analytics.html                  # injected into <head>

# --- Code execution defaults (cells' #| options override these) ---
execute:
  echo: false                                      # hide every source listing
  cache: true                                       # use the _freeze/ cache (default)

# --- Prose linter (opt-in, off by default) ---
prose-lint:
  banned: [utilize, leverage]                       # flag these in prose

# --- Drafts ---
draft: false                                        # true: preview shows it, build excludes it
---
```
